description:
## Problem

The Flash 8 authoring UI has a fully working EaseCurveDialog (packages/authoring-ui/src/EaseCurveDialog.tsx) that allows users to set a custom cubic-Bézier ease on a motion tween. The dialog stores the result in `Frame.motionEaseCurve` (packages/core/src/model/types.ts, line 133). However, this value is silently ignored everywhere downstream:

1. `TweenSpan` (timeline-query.ts line 16) has no `motionEaseCurve` field.
2. `TweenConfig` (tween/types.ts line 23) has no `easeCurve` field.
3. `interpolateTween` (tween/interpolate.ts line 478) always calls `applyEase(linearT, config.ease)` — the integer ease — regardless of whether a custom curve was set.
4. `applyEase` has no cubic-Bézier branch.
5. The SWF compiler (swf/src/compile.ts line 239) passes `ease: span.ease` (always integer 0) and never touches `motionEaseCurve`.

## What to implement

### 1. Add `motionEaseCurve` to TweenSpan

In `getTweenSpans` (timeline-query.ts), read `motionEaseCurve` from the start keyframe and include it in the returned span:

```ts
interface TweenSpan {
  readonly startFrame: number;
  readonly endFrame: number;
  readonly tweenType: "motion" | "shape";
  readonly ease: number;
  readonly motionEaseCurve?: EaseCurve | null;
}
```

### 2. Add `easeCurve` to TweenConfig

```ts
interface TweenConfig {
  ease: number;
  easeCurve?: EaseCurve | null;
  motionRotate?: ...;
  motionRotateCount?: ...;
}
```

### 3. Implement cubic-Bézier ease in `applyEase`

`EaseCurve` stores `{ x1, y1, x2, y2 }` (CSS cubic-bezier control points). Add a `solveCubicBezier(t, x1, y1, x2, y2): number` function to `interpolate.ts` that, given linear `t`, returns the eased `t` by solving the Bézier X-parametric equation for the corresponding Y value. A standard binary-search or Newton-Raphson approach (as used by browsers for `cubic-bezier()`) is correct here.

In `applyEase`, when `easeCurve` is present and non-null, use the cubic-Bézier solver instead of the exponential formula.

### 4. Pass motionEaseCurve through the call stack

In `timeline-query.ts` `getInterpolatedDisplayObjects`, pass `easeCurve: span.motionEaseCurve` to the `TweenConfig`.

### 5. SWF compilation: emit correct ease value

In `swf/src/compile.ts`, when a motion tween span has `motionEaseCurve`, emit the AS3/Flash CustomEase SWF encoding if supported, or at minimum emit the nearest integer ease approximation rather than always 0. (Note: Flash 8 SWF format has no custom-ease tag; the correct approximation is to sample the curve at t=0.5 and convert to the -100..100 range.)

## Acceptance criteria

- A motion tween with a custom ease curve (set via EaseCurveDialog) visually animates on the authoring canvas using the Bézier curve instead of linear motion.
- Unit tests: `applyEase` with a `{ x1:0.42, y1:0, x2:1, y2:1 }` ("Ease In") curve returns values consistent with CSS `cubic-bezier(0.42,0,1,1)` at t=0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.
- Round-trip: `motionEaseCurve` survives FLA save/load unchanged (already works since serialize.ts JSON-serializes the whole doc; add a round-trip test).
- `TweenConfig.easeCurve` overrides the integer `ease` when non-null; integer ease is used as fallback.
